WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
In web handlers, validation should happen before request data reaches business services.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.280
Body validation checks the decoded payload.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:15.420
Query and route parameter validation checks IDs, filters, page sizes, sort options, and flags.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:26.640
Header and cookie validation checks formats, lengths, and expected presence before those values influence authentication, session handling, localization, or routing decisions.

5
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:31.380
Form submissions and multipart uploads add field, file, and metadata checks.

6
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:42.460
Middleware-level validation is useful for broad concerns such as body size limits, content type checks, authentication header shape, and common request metadata.

7
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.440
Handler-level validation remains necessary because each endpoint has a different contract.

8
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:58.980
A profile update, an administrative change, a search request, and a file upload may all use HTTP, but they should not accept the same fields, limits, or combinations of values.

9
00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:04.860
Libraries such as go-playground/validator can help centralize common validation rules for structs.

10
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.440
They are useful when applied consistently, but they should not become a substitute for thinking about the use case.

11
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:15.860
Some rules are structural, such as required fields or maximum length.

12
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:26.200
Other rules are contextual, such as whether a user can update a tenant, whether a workflow transition is allowed, or whether a value is compatible with current account state.

13
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.960
Safe error responses should help legitimate clients without revealing unnecessary internal detail.

14
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.260
It is reasonable to say that a required field is missing or a value is out of range.

15
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:46.360
It is not reasonable to return stack traces, SQL errors, secrets, full request bodies, or internal object details.

16
00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:55.740
Client-side validation improves usability, but server-side validation is the security control because clients can be bypassed, modified, or out of date.
